{
 "_note": "The <api> block conformance corpus (/web/05) — every runtime (TS kernel, Kotlin ApiBlock.kt, Swift ApiBlock.swift) runs these cases through ITS block against a seamed fetch + injected clock; no real network, no real timers, total and deterministic. THE LAW ENCODED HERE: (1) auto defaults true for GET, false otherwise; `auto` is a formula. (2) A block refetches when its MATERIALIZED REQUEST (url+method+headers+body, watchKey-compared) changes — not on raw store pokes. (3) Reserved paths <as>.data/.loading/.refreshing/.error{status,message,body}/.fetchedAt; data null until first resolve; stale data SURVIVES a failed refetch. (4) Network errors (status 0) retry per `retry`; http errors do not. (5) send(body) always fires and bypasses cache; every successful non-GET invalidates its surface's whole API LRU and never seeds a mutation response, while failed/aborted mutations leave the LRU intact; refresh() revalidates (bypasses freshness, rewrites cache). (6) cache: default no-store; max-age(d) serves the cache without a call while age<d; swr(f,s) serves cache-only while age<f, cache + background revalidate (refreshing=true) while f<=age<f+s, normal fetch past f+s; cache storage is surface/session-scoped, bounded LRU, and keyed by method+url+watchKey(headers)+watchKey(body)+expect plus the host's cookie identity partition, so authorization headers, decode modes, sessions, and independent surfaces cannot share entries; a fresh cache hit supersedes/cancels older in-flight work before it can overwrite the hit. (7) events: success fires on every resolved-ok request (source cache or network), error after retries exhaust or http failure, message per streamed chunk (a seamed {stream:[…]} response appends each chunk to <as>.data and fires message, then success). (8) dsx.const (networking.md N0): a case may declare `consts` — the app-wide constants plane (App.json `consts`), read as `dsx.const.<name>` from any url/headers/body/auto expression; present consts materialize, an ABSENT const is typed-null (interpolates empty), and it is REACTIVE — `dsx.const` lives on the same app-wide store as env/global, so a const change re-materializes the request and refetches, exactly like a surface-var change. (9) <api>-in-component (networking.md N1): a case may declare `item` — the COMPONENT SCOPE that an `<api>` mounted inside a component instance reads (the block's 3rd ctor arg); `attribute.<x>`/bare props resolve against it, so the request materializes from the owning component, not the surface root. Steps: {set:{path,value}} store write + settle · {setConst:{name,value}} mutate a const + republish (dsx.const reactivity) · {call:\"remount\"} dispose the block and mount a fresh instance over the same store (the cache probe — a page revisit) · {settle:true} drain schedulers · {call:\"refresh\"|\"cancel\"} · {send:{…}} body-override fire · {advance: seconds} move the injected clock. expectCalls asserts the seam's request URLs in order; expectEvents the ordered event names. (10) THE DEPENDENCY GRAPH (/web/11, RATIFIED): a case may declare `specs` (an ORDERED list of <api> specs sharing one scope) instead of `spec` — the runner builds the graph over them, mounts EVERY block, and only then starts the runnable ones. An edge exists when a block's url/headers/body expression names another block's `as` as its PATH ROOT, plus the explicit `needs=\"a, b\"` (invisible deps: a session side-effect, an ordering-only constraint). An auto block is GATED (never fires) while any of: an upstream has not resolved ok (reason `unresolved`), an upstream carries an error (reason `upstream-error`), a `needs=` target has not resolved (`unresolved`), or an interpolation landing in url/headers/body evaluates to null (`missing-value`). Value-presence gating covers plain variables too; the opt-out is the grammar's own `||` default. AMBIENT PLANES ARE NEVER HOLES — `dsx.const/env/global/screen/source/app/route/cookie/query/params/path` + `platform`/`os` are typed-absent by law (durability P4), so an absent const interpolates empty and does NOT gate. Exposed: `<as>.status` (`waiting|loading|refreshing|ready|error`) and `<as>.blockedBy` ([{name,reason}], [] when runnable); `<as>.loading` stays TRUE while `waiting` because from the user's seat it is loading. An upstream refetch producing identical data does NOT refire dependents (the materialized request is unchanged); changed data does. A cycle, or a `needs=` naming an unknown block, is a DECLARED error — every block in it publishes error {status:-3, message:\"cycle\"|\"unknown-needs\"} and issues no request. Manual send() ignores gating but not holes: it returns {ok:false, error:{kind:\"incomplete-input\", missing:[…]}} without firing (the runner stores the last send envelope at `sendResult`). (11) DECLARED TRANSPORT CONTROLS (networking.md N2): `stream` (declared streaming, no content-type sniffing), `timeout` (ms), `redirect` (follow|error), `encode` (json|text|form|multipart — the request encoding; multipart materializes `parts` sorted by field name), `via=\"server\"` (the proxy route: the request is rewritten to <base>/dsx/api/<as>?u=<encoded target>, never the secret-bearing target), and PROGRESS — a response envelope may carry `progress:[{direction,loaded,total}]`, each publishing `<as>.progress` and firing a `progress` event BEFORE the terminal settle. Steps/expectations added for these: `responsesByUrl` (a per-URL response queue — order-insensitive across sync and async runtimes), `target` on a `call`/`send` step (which block in a `specs` case), and `expectRequests` (per-call dotted-path subset assertions over the MATERIALIZED request).",
